A||||6||MikeFD3S||Considering buying the Fiero - advice?||||5||
Z||000000||MikeFD3S||09-23-2001||06:54 AM||||Hi everyone, I'm considering buying a Fiero and need some tips and other general information.  <P>I currently own a modded 3rd Gen RX7 with 74k miles and it's running perfectly (lots of preventative maintenance)  I'm gonna need to get a decent daily driver since the project FD is getting ludicrously fast, loud, illegal, and uneconomical for every day use.<P>I'm considering a 2nd gen rx7, an AE86  (drift car), or possibly a V6 Fiero.  I owned a 97 Grand AM SE v-6, but it crapped out at 81k miles and suffered from a lot of poor build quality (interior/electrical/engine)<P>I've always liked the Fiero since I was younger, and have seen/heard a few Fieros in my city that have done the V-8 conversion.  I have a lot of questions, so please bear with me:<P>- What kind of 0-60/1320 times can be had with the stock V6 Fiero<BR>- How reliable are they?  Good for a daily driver?<BR>- How much maintenance do they require? (Im a DIY person)<BR>- Anything I should look out for when searching for a V6 Fiero?<BR>- How reliable is the V8 conversion<BR>- How FAST are the V8 Fieros  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Right now my car is running high 12's/low 13's in the 1320, and I'm aiming for a car thats also a bit speedy, fun to drive and reliable (14's-15s)  I'd also like to modify it without burning a hole in my pocket. The 7 already does that for me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">     <P>Any help/tips you guys could provide for this rotorhead would be greatly apreciated<P>------------------<BR>-----------------------<BR>1993 Mazda RX7 R1<BR>A'pexi GT spec catback<BR>A'pexi Power Intake<BR>A'pexi PFC<BR>ACT Street Strip Clutch<BR>HKS Downpipe||||reg||5
Z||000001||Monkeyman||09-23-2001||07:24 AM||||I have absolutely no idea about 1/4 mile times, but Fieros are as reliable as any 15 year old car.  They definitely require some maintenance, but it's usually minor stuff (fixing headlight motors, door bushings, suspension stuff, etc).  If you're a DIY type of guy, you shouldn't have to spend a ton of money on maintenance.  Other than a few model specific parts, most parts are available either through a dealer, from a boneyard or through a forum such as this one.  The only difference I can think of between the V6s was that the '88 was internally balanced.  I've never had an '88 so I don't know if that makes much of a difference.  Alot of it all depends on the previous owner(s).  My '86 GT has almost 200k miles on it and she still runs (although she's getting mighty tired).  Look for a lower mileage, 1 or 2 owner car.  Unless you plan on swapping out the motor, look for a good motor before a good body.  Body panels are easy to replace (they all bolt on) and there were only a few color choices from the factory so the chances are good that you could find another fender in the same factory color.  Just bolt it on and go.  <P>V8 reliability depends on who installs it, the kind of driving you do and how much quality went into the V8 build up.  You might want to contact Archie on the V8 thing.  He's pretty well known as the V8 king.  I'm sure he could tell you more about V8 swaps than you can remember.<P>BTW--Welcome to PFF.  Stick around a while.  We can pretty much answer any questions you have.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||09-23-2001||10:20 AM||||Seems like I remember the manual 2.8s run in the 15's. Not sure about the auto trannies.<BR>We have a member (1FST2M6) who has a 2.8/auto trans, that runs 12.9's with NOS. Not sure what all he's done, but he's spent a lot of time and money. <BR>There is a "search" link at the top of the page. Look around. You'll find what you need, here.<BR>Welcome to the Forum!<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 09-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Brandon86SE||09-23-2001||12:07 PM||||I have a stock 86SE with the 2.8L V6, and a 4spd manual.  Last week at the track I ran a 15.3, and I'd guesstimate my 0-60 at around 6.5-7.  A little minor tweaking, and remembering to take out my spare tire/jack before going to the track :P should put me in the 14's.  Also, I'm way above sea level.  With the conversion I ran a 14.2.  I'd go with the Fiero, lots of great, cheap mods to do, very reliable (had mine 2 years with only minor maintenance things), and I don't know if you've ever driven one, but if not..... get in one, drive it, and you'll never be Fieroless again!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Brandon<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Field_Front_(sig)_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||AL||09-23-2001||03:02 PM||||as been said before, the fiero is old, but with the right attitude going in, you will find that this car will grow on you.<BR>if you do find a car you like, check it out carefully, fixs what has to be fixed and this car will treat you right,i've had mine for 2 yrs now,i redid the heads and changed out belts, hoses, sensors, all fluids, and i haven't had 1 problem yet,the only problem it does give me,is that it begs you to drive it alot.welcome and good luck. <BR><B>AL</B> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/AL_sig.gif"> <BR>84 INDY 4 speed (stole it) 250$<BR>86 GT<BR>89 ws6 GTA<P>------------------<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||MikeFD3S||09-23-2001||05:28 PM||||Thanks for the warm welcome guys, at first i was half expecting to get flamed for being an import guy...but cool, I'll search around the forum<P>btw, hows insurance on these?||||reg||1
